Principle of Insurable Interest: According to this
principle, the insured must have an insurable
interest in the subject matter of insurance policy.
Without interest taking an insurance policy is a
gamble and fraudulent activity and law does not
permit it. For example, a person can take life
insurance policy for his spouse or for his/her
children but he/she cannot take insurance policy
for a stranger. In case of fire insurance, the
insured must suffer a financial loss if the subject
matter is damaged to prove the interest or he must
be the owner of the subject matter.
