Principle of Causa Proxima: According to this principle, the
cause or reason for the loss must be related to the subject matter
of the insurance contract. If loss is due to some other cause, then
the insurer can deny to pay the compensation.
For Example
If a person has taken marine insurance policy for sending the wheat bags and
on the way if a rat spoiled the wheat then no compensation will be given
because under marine insurance the cause of loss should be sea perils and not
the rat. On the other hand, if the rat makes a hole in the ship through which
water enters and spoils the wheat compensation will be given because the loss
of wheat is due to sea water.
